<h2>How pipe bursting works</h2>
<p>Pipe bursting is a replacement, not a repair. We dig one pit near the house and another at the far end of the run, usually near the main or at a point where the line can be reached. A steel cable or rod string is fed through the old sewer between the two pits. On the end of it goes a cone-shaped bursting head, and behind the head we attach the new pipe.</p>
<p>As the head is pulled through, it breaks the old pipe into fragments and pushes them out into the surrounding soil. The new line follows directly behind, filling the path the old one left. When the head reaches the far pit, the new sewer is already in place from end to end. The connections are made at each pit, the camera goes through, and the pits are backfilled and compacted.</p>
<p>The ground between the pits is never opened. Lawns, trees, walks, driveways and, in many cases, the street stay where they are.</p>

<h2>Why fused HDPE</h2>
<p>The new pipe is high-density polyethylene. Before the pull, lengths of it are joined by heat fusion, which melts the ends together into one continuous piece. There is no gasket, no coupling and no glued joint anywhere along the run.</p>
<p>That matters in Colorado for two reasons. First, the most common way Front Range sewers fail is at the joints, where swelling and shrinking clay pulls sections apart and tree roots slip inside. A line without joints removes both problems. Second, polyethylene is flexible. It tolerates a bit of ground movement without cracking, which is the kind of movement bentonite-rich soils deliver year after year.</p>

<h2>When bursting is the right call</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pipe that is too far gone to line.</strong> Crushed clay tile, cast iron with sections missing, or pipe that has partly collapsed.</li>
  <li><strong>Deformed Orangeburg.</strong> Fiber pipe from the postwar decades that has sagged into an oval cannot hold a liner, but it breaks readily ahead of a bursting head.</li>
  <li><strong>Shifted or offset sections.</strong> Where a joint has stepped too far for a liner to bridge, bursting can often pull a smooth new line through the same corridor.</li>
  <li><strong>Lines under trees.</strong> In Thornton we burst 48 feet of cast iron that ran between mature trees, without trenching through their roots.</li>
  <li><strong>A dig that went wrong.</strong> In Boulder we replaced 75 feet of cast iron by bursting after another contractor had started excavating and then walked away.</li>
</ul>
<p>We also used bursting to replace 55 feet of root-cracked cast iron beneath a Denver home that was about 70 years old. The owners kept their yard and their trees.</p>

<h2>When bursting is not the right call</h2>
<p>Bursting pushes fragments of the old pipe outward, and that displacement needs somewhere to go. We rule it out or rethink the plan when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The line runs tight against a foundation wall, a footing, or another utility such as gas or water in the same corridor.</li>
  <li>The sewer passes under a structure where soil movement could cause damage.</li>
  <li>The line has a long belly or has lost grade. A bursting head tends to follow the existing path, so a sag can carry into the new line. Some sags can be corrected by setting grade at the pits; a long one usually cannot.</li>
  <li>Shallow granite or bedrock, which shows up in foothill neighborhoods, leaves nowhere for the old pipe to go.</li>
</ul>
<p>In those cases we recommend a <a href="/sewer-line-replacement/">spot repair or conventional replacement</a> and explain why. Utility locates are called in before any pit is opened, and we compare the marks against what our camera and locator show.</p>

<h2>Bursting versus lining</h2>
<p>Both keep the yard intact, and they solve different problems. <a href="/cipp-pipe-lining/">CIPP lining</a> builds a new pipe inside one that still holds its shape and slope, usually from a single access point. Bursting brings in an entirely new pipe and can handle damage that lining cannot, at the cost of two pits and a bit more time. When a camera shows both kinds of damage on one lateral, we sometimes combine methods.</p>

<h2>The Colorado specifics</h2>
<h3>Depth</h3>
<p>Laterals here are buried below frost depth, and the pit near the main can be deep, particularly when the main sits in the middle of the street. Pits are shored as needed, and we plan their locations around what you want protected.</p>

<h3>Ownership to the main</h3>
<p>In most Front Range cities and districts, owners are responsible for the full lateral, including the tap and the stretch under the street. If the damage extends that far, the replacement has to as well. Bursting can often carry the new line under the road without cutting the whole lane.</p>

<h3>Local rules</h3>
<p>Cities and special sanitation districts each set their own requirements for permits, pipe materials, connections at the main and inspection. We confirm them for your address before we schedule, so the new line passes on the first visit.</p>

<h2>What to expect</h2>
<p>Most residential bursting jobs take one to two days, including excavation of the pits, the pull, the connections and backfill. Your sewer is out of service for part of that time, and we tell you in advance when. At the end you get camera footage of the new line. For the full job sequence see <a href="/our-process/">our process</a>, or check the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.</p>
